## Proof

Let $p \colon D^2 \to X$ be a covering map, where $D^2 = \{x \in \mathbb{R}^2 : \|x\| \le 1\}$ is the closed unit disk.

**Step 1. $D^2$ is the universal cover of $X$.**

The closed disk $D^2$ is compact, path-connected, locally path-connected, and simply connected (in fact contractible). Since $p$ is a covering map and $D^2$ is path-connected, the base $X = p(D^2)$ is path-connected; being locally homeomorphic to $D^2$, it is locally path-connected and semi-locally simply connected. A covering whose total space is simply connected is the universal cover, so $D^2$ is the universal cover of $X$.

**Step 2. The deck transformation group acts freely on $D^2$.**

Let $G = \operatorname{Aut}(p)$ be the group of deck transformations (homeomorphisms $g \colon D^2 \to D^2$ with $p \circ g = p$). A standard fact of covering-space theory: a deck transformation that fixes even one point is the identity. Indeed, the set $\{e \in D^2 : g(e) = e\}$ is both open and closed in $D^2$ (it is closed by continuity, and open because around any fixed point one can choose an evenly covered neighborhood on which $g$ must agree with the identity sheet). Since $D^2$ is connected, this set is either empty or all of $D^2$. Hence every non-identity element of $G$ has **no** fixed point: $G$ acts freely on $D^2$.

Because $D^2$ is the universal cover, $G$ also acts transitively on each fiber, and $X \cong D^2 / G$.

**Step 3. Brouwer forces $G$ to be trivial.**

Take any $g \in G$. As a deck transformation, $g$ is in particular a continuous map $g \colon D^2 \to D^2$. By the **Brouwer fixed point theorem**, every continuous self-map of the closed disk has a fixed point, so there exists $e \in D^2$ with $g(e) = e$. By the freeness established in Step 2, this implies $g = \operatorname{id}_{D^2}$.

Thus $G = \{\operatorname{id}\}$.

**Step 4. Conclusion.**

Since the deck group is trivial, the quotient $D^2/G$ is just $D^2$ itself, and the covering map $p \colon D^2 \to X \cong D^2/G$ is a one-sheeted covering, i.e. a homeomorphism. Therefore

$$X \cong D^2.$$

The only topological space (up to homeomorphism) that admits a covering map from the closed unit disk is the closed unit disk itself.

